SUMMARY:Congested Crossing Pedestrian Traffic Flow: Dispersion vs. Transport in Crowded Areas
DESCRIPTION:This study investigates the complex dynamic interactions between two types of populations coexisting within a shared space. We propose both theoretical and numerical approaches to analyze scenarios where one population (population 1) must traverse a territory occupied by another (population 2)\, requiring strategies to mitigate overcrowding due to spatial limitations. To capture these interactions\,\nhttps://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/16778/
LOCATION:XLIM
URL:https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/16778/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7e2e978d6c6f@paris-academique
DTSTAMP:20260617T085855Z
DTSTART:20260619T110000
DTEND:20260619T113000
SUMMARY:Transport\, Correction\, and Reaction Dynamics in Congested Pedestrian Flows
DESCRIPTION:This work extends an initial model of pedestrian crossing in a congested area by introducing richer dynamics for the two interacting populations. We successively consider transport mechanisms\, sandpile‑type correction dynamics\, and a reaction coupling\, allowing us to capture a wider diversity of collective dynamics in high‑density conditions.\nhttps://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/16778/
LOCATION:XLIM
URL:https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/16778/
